  "measured": "the line and the rise, from the WA DNR cascades_north_wali_2023 clip: the foot is where the ground first gains 1.0 m over the next 3.0 m going east, which is the toe of the built work rather than the lip of the apron, and the top is the ground 3.0 m back from it. The block is 200 mm on the course and 400 on the face, counted off the photographs against a loose block lying on the concrete.",
  "not_measured": "the wall face itself. A lidar flight looks down and a riser is vertical, so no flight will ever hold it. The setback of 0.06 m a course is the ordinary batter for this block and is not measured here. Neither is where the wall stops and starts: it is drawn unbroken along the frontage, and the photographs show it broken through in places and a stair cut down through it. Where the ground rises past 12 courses over the concrete the rest is carried as bank_above_m and no wall is claimed for it. The line and the height are run through a 9-station median along the wall first, because a wall is built continuous and coursed and the raw reading jumps with the interpolated ground behind it.",
